# 1. Your first curve (interactive)

```elixir
# Local development copy: uses this repository instead of the Hex package.
Mix.install(
  [
    {:curves, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "../../"), env: :dev},
    {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ],
  config_path: :curves,
  lockfile: :curves
)
```

## Welcome!

This is the first of four short, hands-on chapters. You'll make a curve, move along it, and race some
curves against each other. Evaluate each cell in order (or click **Evaluate all** at the top), then play
with the sliders and buttons.

Every picture is drawn by `Curves.Svg`. In each one:

* the **blue line** is the curve,
* the **orange dots** are the points that shape it,
* the **dashed lines** show which point pulls on which part of the curve,
* the **purple dots** are points we asked for,
* and the grid shows the real `x` and `y` values.

## Make a curve

The quickest way to get a curve is to pick a ready-made one by name:

```elixir
curve = Curves.define_bezier(:ease_in_out)

curve
|> Curves.Svg.render(width: 480, height: 360)
|> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
```

## Moving along a curve with `t`

Think of the curve as a road. `t` says how far along the road you are: `0.0` is the start, `1.0` is the
end. `Curves.solve!/2` turns a `t` into an `{x, y}` point:

```elixir
Curves.solve!(curve, 0.5)
```

Now try it yourself. Drag the **t** slider and watch the purple dot travel. Pick a different curve too:
some of them move slowly at first, some rush off, and some even overshoot!

```elixir
names = Enum.map(Curves.Bezier.Predefined.list(), &{&1, inspect(&1)})

form =
  Kino.Control.form(
    [
      name: Kino.Input.select("Curve", names, default: :ease_in_out),
      t: Kino.Input.range("t", min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01, default: 0.3)
    ],
    report_changes: true
  )

frame = Kino.Frame.new()

render = fn %{name: name, t: t} ->
  curve = Curves.define_bezier(name)
  {x, y} = Curves.solve!(curve, t)

  Kino.Layout.grid(
    [
      Kino.Markdown.new(
        "`Curves.solve!(curve, #{t})` → `{#{Float.round(x * 1.0, 3)}, #{Float.round(y * 1.0, 3)}}`"
      ),
      curve
      |> Curves.Svg.render(width: 480, height: 360, markers: [{x, y}], title: inspect(name))
      |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
    ],
    columns: 1
  )
end

Kino.Frame.render(frame, render.(%{name: :ease_in_out, t: 0.3}))
Kino.listen(form, fn %{data: data} -> Kino.Frame.render(frame, render.(data)) end)
Kino.Layout.grid([form, frame], columns: 1)
```

Did you notice that the dot doesn't move at a steady pace? `t` is *time* along the road, not distance.
On `:ease_in_out` the dot starts slowly, rushes through the middle and slows down at the end. That's
exactly what makes it feel smooth in an animation.

## Many points at once with `take`

To draw a curve, or move something along it, you usually want many points. `Curves.take!/2` gives you
`n` of them, evenly spaced in `t`:

```elixir
Curves.take!(curve, 5)
```

Slide **n** to ask for more or fewer points:

```elixir
form =
  Kino.Control.form(
    [n: Kino.Input.range("n", min: 1, max: 40, step: 1, default: 10)],
    report_changes: true
  )

frame = Kino.Frame.new()

render = fn %{n: n} ->
  curve
  |> Curves.Svg.render(width: 480, height: 360, markers: Curves.take!(curve, round(n)))
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end

Kino.Frame.render(frame, render.(%{n: 10}))
Kino.listen(form, fn %{data: data} -> Kino.Frame.render(frame, render.(data)) end)
Kino.Layout.grid([form, frame], columns: 1)
```

`take` starts *after* the start: its points are at `t = 1/n, 2/n, ... 1.0`. Add
`Curves.solve!(curve, 0.0)` if you need the very first point too.

## When things go wrong

`solve!` and `take!` raise an error when something is wrong. Their siblings `solve` and `take` return
`{:ok, result}` or `{:error, reason}` instead. Try a `t` past the end:

```elixir
Curves.solve(curve, 1.5)
```

## The gallery

Here is every ready-made curve. `Curves.Bezier.Predefined.list/0` gives you their names.

```elixir
Curves.Bezier.Predefined.list()
|> Enum.map(fn name ->
  name
  |> Curves.define_bezier()
  |> Curves.Svg.render(width: 220, height: 180, padding: 24, title: inspect(name), show_axes: false)
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end)
|> Kino.Layout.grid(columns: 4)
```

## The easing race 🏁

Easing curves decide *how* something moves from start to finish. Let's race five of them! Each runner
uses the `y` of its curve as "how far along the track am I", for `t` going from `0.0` to `1.0`. They all
start and finish at the same time, but watch how differently they get there.

Press **Start the race!** (as often as you like).

```elixir
runners = [
  {:linear, "#1c7ed6"},
  {:ease_in, "#e8590c"},
  {:ease_out, "#2f9e44"},
  {:ease_in_out, "#7048e8"},
  {:ease_out_back, "#e64980"}
]

# Draws the track at time t: one lane per runner, with a dot at its progress.
render = fn %{t: t} ->
  lanes =
    for {{name, color}, lane} <- Enum.with_index(runners) do
      {_x, progress} = Curves.solve!(Curves.define_bezier(name), t)
      y = 40 + lane * 50
      x = 120 + progress * 440

      """
      <text x="10" y="#{y + 5}" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="14">#{inspect(name)}</text>
      <line x1="120" y1="#{y}" x2="560" y2="#{y}" stroke="#dee2e6" stroke-width="2"/>
      <circle cx="#{x}" cy="#{y}" r="12" fill="#{color}"/>
      """
    end

  Kino.Image.new(
    """
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="600" height="280">
      <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="#ffffff"/>
      <line x1="560" y1="20" x2="560" y2="260" stroke="#212529" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>
      #{lanes}
    </svg>
    """,
    :svg
  )
end

button = Kino.Control.button("Start the race!")
frame = Kino.Frame.new()
Kino.Frame.render(frame, render.(%{t: 0.0}))

Kino.listen(button, fn _event ->
  for step <- 0..60 do
    Kino.Frame.render(frame, render.(%{t: step / 60}))
    Process.sleep(30)
  end
end)

Kino.Layout.grid([button, frame], columns: 1)
```

Who won? Nobody: they all finish at `t = 1.0`. But `:ease_out` sprints off and coasts in,
`:ease_in` starts slowly and speeds up, and `:ease_out_back` overshoots the line and comes back. Try
swapping in other names from the gallery!

